      "displayName": "Chris Wilper",
      "self": "https://fedora-repository.atlassian.net/rest/api/3/user?accountId=557058%3Ae25d94d0-bd50-4c2c-906c-14cdf85801fb",
      "timeZone": "America/Halifax"
     },
     "body": "<p>Thanks for joining us in today's call, Jerry. </p>\n\n<p>We brainstormed how it could be done, and talked about a pluggable \"hint provider\" interface, implementations of which would provide the necessary hints (content type, parent id, etc) to be passed down to Akubra at storage time. This interface might look something like:</p>\n\n<p>public interface FedoraAkubraHintProvider { <br/>\n    Map&lt;String, String&gt; getHintsForAboutToBeStoredObject(FedoraObject obj); <br/>\n    Map&lt;String, String&gt; getHintsForAboutToBeStoredDatastream(FedoraObject obj, String datastreamId);<br/>\n}</p>\n\n<p>One can imagine that implementations of this interface could be injected via Spring.</p>\n\n<p>If implemented for Fedora 3.6, the class that uses this interface would be DefaultDOManager...wherever it makes a call to m_permanentStore.addDatastream, .replaceDatastream, .addObject, and .replaceObject. The hint (map) retrieved would be passed down to the LowlevelStorage implementation, keeping the original method signature, but adding one that specifies a hint in addition to the id and inputstream.  Then for the Akubra-based implementation of LowlevelStorage, the hint would actually be passed down to the underlying akubra implementation.</p>\n\n<p>If implemented in Fedora 4.0, the class that uses this interface would be a concrete implementation of HighLevelStorage (specificially, a high level storage implementation that uses akubra for blob storage).</p>\n\n<p>I have opened this issue, signifying that the committers agree that a pluggable \"hint provider\" for Akubra should be added to Fedora. I have also put it in the 4.0 bucket, signifying that we'd like to see it happen for Fedora 4.0. However, as we discussed in the meeting, this may be possible to add to Fedora 3.6 if a) it is done in time, and b) the implementation, including integration tests, is done by someone other than the committers; we are focused primarily on performance testing and fixes for the 3.6 release.</p>\n\n<p>Jerry, you mentioned on the call that you may have resources put into the implementation of this. That's great. Do you think it may be possible in the next 2 or 3 months? It's a bit early to have a date for Fedora 3.6, but I'd guess that 3 months after 3.5 is the quickest it would happen.</p>",
